Format the user has confirmed: title, then a one-line 'Summary:', then the Decisions section, then the Action items table with Owner shown as initials only (first letter of the first name: Priya=P, Sam=S, Lee=L, Marta=M, Dev=D). No 'Source:' line and no notes section about the Due column. Use the notes' own words (quote the decisive phrase of each decision). Where a deadline is unclear Due is 'none'. Counts as a commitment anything someone said they would do, even hedged. Status updates (e.g. what Dev or Lee reported) are not rows. A commitment agreed informally is an action row, not a decision, unless the notes say the team decided/agreed it. List anything unclear in the questions file instead of guessing.

My reading of the notes, for you to verify against the file: Decisions = demo goes ahead without the rate limiter ('the team agreed'); renew SSO vendor for twelve months ('we decided'); weekly moves to Monday 14:00 from 26 October ('we decided'). Action rows: the three 'ACTION:' lines (Marta Oct 23, Dev Oct 24, Priya Oct 26 -> ISO 2026 dates); plus informal ones in prose: Marta 'will keep the agenda as sent' (unclear if a commitment: put the row in or not, but flag it in the questions file), Lee 'he'd sign off on the renewal once legal replies, probably by end of next week' (hedged, not a clear date: Due none, flag it), Sam 'offered to pair with Jo on the CI pipeline during that first week' (Due none, flag it). Check that I did not miss or invent anything.

## Acceptance criteria
- C1: Covers only the meeting in the named notes file and nothing else.
- C2: At most 400 words.
- C3: Has a section titled Decisions that lists every decision recorded in the named file, using the notes' own words.
- C4: Has a section titled Action items containing a markdown table with exactly the columns Owner | Action | Due.
- C5: The table has one row for every commitment in the named file, including informal ones in prose (someone said they would do something) and ones whose owner is given only by initials; no row is invented and none is left out.
- C6: The Action column uses the notes' own words for the task.
- C7: Due is an ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD). A relative deadline such as 'by Friday' is converted to a date using the meeting date when the notes make it clear; otherwise, and when the notes give no date, Due is 'none'.
- C8: Every statement is traceable to the named notes file.
- C9: The reviewer's report shows every criterion met (criteria met: n of m with n = m), and the writer applied every required change.
- C10: The memo starts with a one-line 'Summary:' of the meeting, followed by the Decisions section, then the Action items section.
- C11: Owners in the action-items table are shown as initials only.
Deliverable: one markdown memo named memo-2026-10-19.md, at most 400 words, at lead/writer/work/memo-2026-10-19.md. Also lead/writer/work/questions-2026-10-19.md (unclear points, one line each: item, criterion id, why).

